---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Sump Pumps",
    "url": "/best-sump-pump/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Battery Backup Sump Pumps",
    "url": "/best-battery-backup-sump-pump/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Sump Pump Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-sump-pump-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Pedestal vs Submersible Sump Pump",
    "url": "/pedestal-vs-submersible-sump-pump/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Drain a Water Heater",
    "url": "/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Vacuum Water with a Shop Vac",
    "url": "/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Shop Vacs",
    "url": "/best-cordless-shop-vac/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Cordless Tool Batteries Explained",
    "url": "/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Ryobi ONE+ Tools",
    "url": "/best-ryobi-one-plus-tools/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Milwaukee vs DeWalt",
    "url": "/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "6 Best Battery Powered Water Pumps in 2026: Cordless & 12V";
const description =
  "Six battery powered water pumps compared: cordless transfer pumps from Milwaukee, DeWalt and Ryobi, 12V RV diaphragm pumps, and a 12V submersible.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-m18-transfer-pump",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 Transfer Pump (2771-20)",
    "category": "Cordless Transfer Pump",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated 480 GPH, among the quickest cordless pumps",
      "18 ft of lift and 75 ft of head per Milwaukee",
      "Brass 3/4 in. garden hose connections",
      "Runs on any M18 battery you already own",
      "Compact enough to carry in one hand"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Bare tool price is high if you are not on M18",
      "Water only: not for fuel, oil or solvents"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pump to buy if you already own M18 batteries and drain things more than once a year.",
    "description": "The Milwaukee 2771-20 is the cordless transfer pump we would hand to anyone who drains water heaters, pool covers, flooded window wells or rain barrels more than once a season. Milwaukee rates it at 480 gallons per hour, with 18 feet of lift and 75 feet of head, and that combination matters more than the headline flow. Lift is how far the pump can pull water up the suction hose from below, which is what lets you leave the pump on dry ground beside a window well instead of standing it in the water. Head is how high it can push water out the other side, which covers getting water up and out of a basement stairwell or over a lip into a drain. The connections are brass 3/4 inch garden hose threads, so any ordinary hose works on both ends and the threads do not strip after a season the way plastic ones can. Milwaukee says an M18 REDLITHIUM XC5.0 pack moves up to 240 gallons per charge, which is about 30 minutes of pumping at full rate and enough to empty four rain barrels or a 50 gallon water heater with plenty to spare. A bigger HIGH OUTPUT pack goes further. The limits are the usual ones for this class: it is for water and water-based liquids, not gasoline, diesel or solvents, and the pump body is not meant to be submerged. Keep it on dry ground and let the suction hose do the reaching. If you are not already on the M18 platform, the cost of a battery and charger makes the DeWalt or Ryobi a better value."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dce050-transfer-pump",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX Transfer Pump (DCE050)",
    "category": "Cordless Transfer Pump",
    "badge": "Most Flow",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated up to 10 GPM with tap water",
      "Up to 300 gallons per 5.0Ah charge per DeWalt",
      "Self-priming",
      "Rated for 60/40 water and propylene glycol mixes",
      "Sold bare (DCE050B) or as a kit (DCE050P1)"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Flow falls to about 4.5 GPM at 45 ft of head",
      "Bulkier than the Milwaukee"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best pick for DeWalt owners and for anyone flushing hydronic or RV systems with glycol.",
    "description": "DeWalt's DCE050 has the biggest peak number in this group: up to 10 gallons per minute with tap water, which works out to roughly 600 gallons per hour at the top of the curve. The more useful figure is the one DeWalt publishes alongside it: about 4.5 gallons per minute at 45 feet of head. That is the honest way to rate a pump, because every pump loses flow as it has to push water higher, and seeing both numbers tells you how this one behaves when you are moving water up out of a basement rather than across a flat lawn. DeWalt says a 5.0Ah 20V MAX battery transfers up to 300 gallons per charge, the most per pack of anything on this list, and the pump is self-priming so it will pull water up a dry suction hose without you filling it first. It is also rated for a 60/40 water and propylene glycol mix, which makes it more interesting than the others for plumbers and RV owners who fill or flush hydronic heating loops and winterize with antifreeze. It comes as the bare DCE050B or the DCE050P1 kit with a 5.0Ah battery and charger. The trade-offs are size, since it is a chunkier unit than the Milwaukee, and the fact that the 10 GPM peak only shows up with a short, low lift and a good battery. Like the others, it is a water pump, not a fuel pump, and the tool body stays out of the water. If you already run DeWalt 20V tools, this is the one to buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-p750-transfer-pump",
    "name": "Ryobi 18V ONE+ Hybrid Transfer Pump (P750)",
    "category": "Cordless Transfer Pump",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Lowest price of the cordless pumps",
      "Rated 325 GPH",
      "Hybrid: runs on a ONE+ battery or a 120V cord",
      "About 200 gallons per 4.0Ah battery per Ryobi",
      "Light and easy to stash"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slower than Milwaukee and DeWalt",
      "Plastic build is less durable on a jobsite"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right buy for homeowners who drain a rain barrel or water heater a few times a year.",
    "description": "Ryobi's P750 is the transfer pump most homeowners actually need. It is rated at 325 gallons per hour, which is slower than the Milwaukee and DeWalt but still empties a 55 gallon rain barrel in about ten minutes and a 40 gallon water heater in under ten once it is cool. The feature that sets it apart is that it is a hybrid: it runs on any Ryobi ONE+ 18V battery, or you can plug it into a 120V outlet with an extension cord and pump for as long as you need without watching a battery gauge. That matters for longer jobs like draining a small above-ground pool or a hot tub, where a single battery would run flat partway through. Ryobi says a ONE+ 4.0Ah battery moves up to 200 gallons per charge, so for one-off chores you can skip the cord entirely. It uses standard garden hose fittings and it is small enough to live on a garage shelf next to the rest of the ONE+ kit. The trade-offs are what you would expect at this price. The housing is plastic, the flow is the lowest of the three cordless pumps, and it is not built for daily contractor abuse. It is also strictly a water pump: do not run fuel through it. Ryobi has a separate, higher-output ONE+ transfer pump in some markets, but the P750 is the widely sold US model. If you already have ONE+ batteries and drain things a handful of times a year, this is the pump to buy, and spending more gets you speed you will rarely use."
  },
  {
    "id": "shurflo-revolution-4008",
    "name": "SHURflo Revolution 4008-101-E65",
    "category": "12V Diaphragm Pump",
    "badge": "Best 12V RV Pump",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "3.0 GPM at 12V DC",
      "55 PSI shut-off pressure",
      "Internal bypass smooths flow at a single tap",
      "Industry-standard RV replacement footprint",
      "Quiet for a diaphragm pump"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Pricier than Seaflo",
      "Pulls up to about 7.5A, so it needs proper wiring and a fuse"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The drop-in 12V pump for an RV, van or cabin water system that you want to forget about.",
    "description": "The SHURflo Revolution 4008 is the pump a lot of RVs leave the factory with, and the one most people buy when the original dies. It is a 12 volt DC diaphragm pump rated at 3.0 gallons per minute with a 55 PSI shut-off, and it uses an internal bypass so the flow stays steady when only one faucet is open instead of pulsing on and off. That bypass is the difference between a pump you notice every time you wash a dish and one you forget is there. Listed current draw is up to about 7.5 amps, which on a 12V battery is roughly 90 watts while it runs. Because a water pump only runs in short bursts, a 100Ah lithium house battery barely notices it: even 30 minutes of total run time a day is under 4Ah. The ports are 1/2 inch male NPSM, the standard RV fitting, so it swaps into most existing systems with the same plumbing. This is not a transfer pump. It is built to keep a pressurized line full and switch on when you open a tap, which also makes it the right choice for a cabin, a tiny house on a cistern, or an off-grid outdoor sink fed from a tank. Wire it with the gauge and fuse the manual calls for and fit a strainer on the inlet, because debris is what kills diaphragm pumps. The 110V version is a different model number, so make sure you order the 12V 4008-101-E65 for a battery system. If you want the best-known name and easy parts, buy this one."
  },
  {
    "id": "seaflo-42-series-pump",
    "name": "SEAFLO 42 Series 12V Diaphragm Pump (3.0 GPM)",
    "category": "12V Diaphragm Pump",
    "badge": "Best Value 12V",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "3.0 GPM at 12V for noticeably less money",
      "Factory set to 55 PSI, adjustable",
      "Self-priming up to about 6 vertical feet",
      "Can run dry briefly without damage",
      "Includes strainer and fittings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Louder and pulsier than the SHURflo at low flow",
      "Quality control is less consistent"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid budget 12V pump for vans, trailers, boats and garden tank sprayers.",
    "description": "SEAFLO's 42 Series is the value alternative to the SHURflo and the pump you see in a lot of van builds, small boats and DIY water tank setups. The common 12V model is rated at 3.0 gallons per minute with the pressure switch set to 55 PSI at the factory, and the switch can be adjusted if you need a lower shut-off for older plumbing or a higher one for a longer run. It is self-priming to about 6 vertical feet and can run dry for a short time without damage, which is forgiving if your tank runs empty before you notice. Listed maximum draw is about 7.5 amps, similar to the SHURflo, so the battery math is the same: a few amp-hours a day for normal use. In practice the SEAFLO is a little louder and a little more prone to pulsing at a slow trickle, because it lacks the SHURflo's bypass design; adding a small accumulator tank in the line smooths that out and saves the pump from short-cycling. Owners commonly report good life when it is mounted on a soft pad and fed through a strainer, and earlier failures when it is bolted straight to a hollow floor and starved. Some listings around this product quote different pressures and amp figures, so buy from a seller that names the exact model number. For a first van build, a pull-behind trailer or a 12V sprayer setup on a garden tank, this is the sensible place to start, and you can step up to the SHURflo later if the noise bothers you."
  },
  {
    "id": "rule-1100-bilge-pump",
    "name": "Rule 1100 GPH 12V Submersible Pump (27D)",
    "category": "12V Submersible Pump",
    "badge": "Best 12V Submersible",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Submersible, so nothing to prime",
      "Draws about 3.7A at 12V",
      "Runs for hours off a small 12V battery",
      "Marine-grade build tolerates being wet",
      "Cheap and widely stocked"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1100 GPH is an open-flow rating; real flow drops fast with height",
      "Needs DIY wiring, a fuse and a 1-1/8 in. hose"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to pump water out of a hole, a hot tub or a boat with just a 12V battery.",
    "description": "Rule's 1100 GPH bilge pump was designed to keep boats afloat, but it has a second life as the most practical battery operated submersible pump you can buy for odd jobs. Drop it in a flooded window well, a hot tub, a pond, a stock tank or a pool cover puddle, clip it to a 12V battery and it pushes water out through a 1-1/8 inch hose with nothing to prime. Listed draw is about 3.7 amps at 12 volts, roughly 45 watts, so a 20Ah lithium battery runs it for around four to five hours and a car battery or a portable power station with a 12V socket runs it far longer. That efficiency is the whole point. The catch is the rating. Bilge pump numbers are open-flow figures with no height to climb, and flow drops steeply as head increases; push water up 6 to 10 feet and you should expect a fraction of the label, not 1,100 gallons an hour. It is excellent for moving water sideways or up a short lift and poor for lifting it out of a deep pit. You will also need to do a little wiring: alligator clips, an inline fuse sized to the manual and a long enough lead so the battery stays dry and well away from the water. The 27D is the manual version with no float switch, which is what you want for a temporary job; Rule also sells automatic versions. Do not treat this as a permanent basement pump. For that, see the sump pump picks linked below."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Battery Powered Water Pumps",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-battery-powered-water-pump/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best battery powered water pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For draining and moving water, the Milwaukee M18 Transfer Pump is the best all-rounder, with 480 GPH and strong lift and head ratings. The Ryobi P750 is the best value for occasional homeowner jobs. For RV or off-grid running water, a 12V diaphragm pump like the SHURflo Revolution 4008 is the right tool instead, because transfer pumps cannot pressurize a faucet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a battery water pump run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Cordless transfer pumps run roughly 20 to 40 minutes per battery at full flow. Milwaukee rates the M18 at up to 240 gallons per XC5.0 pack, DeWalt rates up to 300 gallons per 5.0Ah pack, and Ryobi rates up to 200 gallons per 4.0Ah pack. A 12V bilge pump drawing about 3.7 amps runs four to five hours on a 20Ah battery."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a battery water pump empty a rain barrel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and it is one of the best uses for one. A 55 gallon barrel takes roughly ten minutes with the Ryobi P750 and less with the Milwaukee or DeWalt, using about a quarter of a battery. Put a screen on the suction hose so leaves and grit do not reach the impeller, and keep the inlet off the sediment at the bottom."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size 12 volt water pump do I need for an RV?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most RVs and vans, a 3.0 GPM pump at around 50 to 55 PSI is right. It runs a faucet and a shower head together without draining the tank too fast. Match the port size and voltage of the pump you are replacing first. Bigger pumps cycle harder and can stress older RV plumbing without improving the shower."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a cordless transfer pump as a sump pump backup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not reliably. It has no float switch, needs someone to start it, and runs only about half an hour per battery. A storm can keep a sump pump running all night. Use a dedicated battery backup sump system with its own float and deep-cycle battery, and keep the cordless pump for cleanup and draining jobs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a battery water pump pump hot water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only once it has cooled. Most cordless transfer pumps are not rated for very hot water, and scalding water softens hose fittings and seals and can burn you if a connection pops off. When draining a water heater or hot tub, turn the heat off and let the water cool for several hours before you start pumping."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cheap cordless water pumps from unknown brands worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Many marketplace pumps quote flow numbers that are optimistic or simply wrong, sometimes confusing gallons per hour with gallons per minute, and parts and warranty support can be nonexistent. If you already own a battery platform, the matching brand-name pump is often only a little more and comes with a real spec sheet and a warranty."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "battery powered water pump",
      "battery operated water pump",
      "water pump battery powered",
      "cordless transfer pump",
      "12 volt dc water pump",
      "rain barrel pump"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Battery Powered Water Pumps</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Cordless transfer pumps for draining and moving water, 12V diaphragm pumps for RVs and off-grid taps, and a 12V submersible, matched to the job each one actually does well.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-m18-transfer-pump"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Which Type Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Three Types</a>
          <a href="#flow-head" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Flow Rate and Head</a>
          <a href="#runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Battery Runtime Math</a>
          <a href="#uses" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Matching Pump to Job</a>
          <a href="#rv-offgrid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. 12V for RV and Off-Grid</a>
          <a href="#not-a-sump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Not a Sump Pump</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Hoses, Priming and Filters</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Care and Storage</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A battery powered water pump is three different tools sold under one name. A cordless transfer pump moves a lot of water a short distance and runs off the same pack as your drill. A 12V diaphragm pump pressurizes a faucet in an RV or cabin. A 12V submersible sits in the water and pushes it out. Buy the wrong type and it will either be painfully slow or will not prime at all, so pick the job first and the pump second.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Our Top Battery Water Pump Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We compared rated flow, head and lift, gallons per charge, fittings, duty cycle and owner reports, and we stuck to brands with real spec sheets and parts support. The cheap no-name cordless pumps on marketplaces quote flow numbers that rarely survive contact with a garden hose, so they are not here. Three picks are cordless tool pumps, two are 12V pressure pumps for RV and off-grid water, and one is a 12V submersible for pumping out a hole or a boat. Battery submersible utility pumps from major brands are still thin on the ground in the US, which is why this list stops at six instead of padding it.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
