        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Three Types of Battery Water Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every confused review of a battery powered water pump comes from someone who bought the wrong type. The three categories look similar on a product page, but they are engineered for completely different jobs, and none of them does the other two jobs well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless transfer pumps</h3>

          <p>
            A transfer pump is a small impeller pump with a garden hose fitting on each side. You run a suction hose into the water, a discharge hose to wherever it is going, and the pump moves a lot of water in a hurry. The Milwaukee, DeWalt and Ryobi models here are all transfer pumps that run off the same battery packs as their drills, which is the main reason to buy one: if you already own the batteries, the pump is cheap. They are for emptying things, not for pressurizing a faucet, and the tool body is not meant to sit in the water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V diaphragm pumps</h3>

          <p>
            A diaphragm pump like the SHURflo or SEAFLO is what pressurizes the water in an RV, a van, a boat or a cabin with a tank. It moves only about 3 gallons a minute, but it builds 50 to 60 PSI and shuts itself off when the line is full, then switches back on when you open a tap. That makes it a terrible tool for draining a pool cover and the only right tool for running a shower off a tank. These are the pumps people mean when they search for a 12 volt DC water pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V submersible pumps</h3>

          <p>
            A submersible sits in the water and pushes it out. Battery versions are mostly 12V bilge pumps like the Rule, plus a growing number of 18V tool-battery stick pumps from brands with patchy spec sheets. They need no priming and they handle a puddle that a transfer pump would struggle to pull from, but most of them lose flow quickly when they have to push water uphill. Pick the type first, using the table below, and the model choice becomes easy.
          </p>

