        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery powered water pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For draining and moving water, the Milwaukee M18 Transfer Pump is the best all-rounder, with 480 GPH and strong lift and head ratings. The Ryobi P750 is the best value for occasional homeowner jobs. For RV or off-grid running water, a 12V diaphragm pump like the SHURflo Revolution 4008 is the right tool instead, because transfer pumps cannot pressurize a faucet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery water pump run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cordless transfer pumps run roughly 20 to 40 minutes per battery at full flow. Milwaukee rates the M18 at up to 240 gallons per XC5.0 pack, DeWalt rates up to 300 gallons per 5.0Ah pack, and Ryobi rates up to 200 gallons per 4.0Ah pack. A 12V bilge pump drawing about 3.7 amps runs four to five hours on a 20Ah battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery water pump empty a rain barrel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best uses for one. A 55 gallon barrel takes roughly ten minutes with the Ryobi P750 and less with the Milwaukee or DeWalt, using about a quarter of a battery. Put a screen on the suction hose so leaves and grit do not reach the impeller, and keep the inlet off the sediment at the bottom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size 12 volt water pump do I need for an RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most RVs and vans, a 3.0 GPM pump at around 50 to 55 PSI is right. It runs a faucet and a shower head together without draining the tank too fast. Match the port size and voltage of the pump you are replacing first. Bigger pumps cycle harder and can stress older RV plumbing without improving the shower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a cordless transfer pump as a sump pump backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. It has no float switch, needs someone to start it, and runs only about half an hour per battery. A storm can keep a sump pump running all night. Use a dedicated battery backup sump system with its own float and deep-cycle battery, and keep the cordless pump for cleanup and draining jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery water pump pump hot water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only once it has cooled. Most cordless transfer pumps are not rated for very hot water, and scalding water softens hose fittings and seals and can burn you if a connection pops off. When draining a water heater or hot tub, turn the heat off and let the water cool for several hours before you start pumping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap cordless water pumps from unknown brands worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Many marketplace pumps quote flow numbers that are optimistic or simply wrong, sometimes confusing gallons per hour with gallons per minute, and parts and warranty support can be nonexistent. If you already own a battery platform, the matching brand-name pump is often only a little more and comes with a real spec sheet and a warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

