        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pick the job before the pump. For draining barrels, heaters, hot tubs and pool covers, a cordless transfer pump on a battery platform you already own is the answer: the Milwaukee M18 if you want the fastest and toughest, the DeWalt if you are a DeWalt shop or work with glycol, and the Ryobi P750 if you drain things a few times a year and want the plug-in option for long jobs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For running water from a tank, buy a 12V diaphragm pump: the SHURflo Revolution for the quietest, best-supported option, or the SEAFLO 42 Series to save money. For pumping out a hole or a boat with just a battery, the Rule 1100 is cheap and efficient. And if the real worry is a flooding basement, none of these is the fix; a proper sump pump with an automatic battery backup is.
